From: LuneOS <team@webos-ports.org>
Date: Tue, 15 Sep 2026 10:00:00 +0200
Subject: [PATCH] ipa: rkisp1: cproc: take brightness/contrast/saturation from
 the tuning file

ColorProcessing::init() already receives the tuning data but discards it:

    int ColorProcessing::init(IPAContext &context,
                              [[maybe_unused]] const YamlObject &tuningData)

so the three parameters it manages are pinned to their compile-time defaults
(brightness 0.0, contrast 1.0, saturation 1.0) and can only be changed by an
application setting the corresponding controls on every request. A sensor whose
tuning wants a different starting point has no way to express it.

Read the defaults from the tuning file, falling back to the existing constants
when the keys are absent, and use them both for the reported ControlInfo
defaults and for the values programmed at configure() time. Values are clamped
to the same ranges the controls advertise.

This matters for sensors with no Ccm data. The rkisp1 tuning files ship no
colour correction matrices at all - none of the seven in src/ipa/rkisp1/data
contains one - so camera RGB reaches the display uncorrected and saturated
colours come out muted; on a PinePhone Pro a bright red object renders as a
dark orange. A CCM is the right fix and needs a Macbeth chart, but until that
data exists a per-sensor saturation default is a useful lever. Measured with
the saturation control on a PinePhone Pro (ov8858), chroma energy scales
linearly with it:

    saturation 0.3 -> 0.32x    1.0 -> 1.00x    1.8 -> 1.77x

Note this scales chroma along its existing hue and so does not correct a hue
error; it is a mitigation, not a substitute for Ccm.

Upstream-Status: Pending
---
diff -ruN cp72_a/src/ipa/rkisp1/algorithms/cproc.cpp cp72_b/src/ipa/rkisp1/algorithms/cproc.cpp
--- a/src/ipa/rkisp1/algorithms/cproc.cpp
+++ b/src/ipa/rkisp1/algorithms/cproc.cpp
@@ -88,14 +88,23 @@
 /**
  * \copydoc libcamera::ipa::Algorithm::init
  */
-int ColorProcessing::init(IPAContext &context,
-			  [[maybe_unused]] const ValueNode &tuningData)
+int ColorProcessing::init(IPAContext &context, const ValueNode &tuningData)
 {
 	auto &cmap = context.ctrlMap;
 
-	cmap[&controls::Brightness] = ControlInfo(-1.0f, 0.993f, kDefaultBrightness);
-	cmap[&controls::Contrast] = ControlInfo(0.0f, 1.993f, kDefaultContrast);
-	cmap[&controls::Saturation] = ControlInfo(0.0f, 1.993f, kDefaultSaturation);
+	defaultBrightness_ =
+		std::clamp(tuningData["brightness"].get<float>(kDefaultBrightness),
+			   -1.0f, 0.993f);
+	defaultContrast_ =
+		std::clamp(tuningData["contrast"].get<float>(kDefaultContrast),
+			   0.0f, 1.993f);
+	defaultSaturation_ =
+		std::clamp(tuningData["saturation"].get<float>(kDefaultSaturation),
+			   0.0f, 1.993f);
+
+	cmap[&controls::Brightness] = ControlInfo(-1.0f, 0.993f, defaultBrightness_);
+	cmap[&controls::Contrast] = ControlInfo(0.0f, 1.993f, defaultContrast_);
+	cmap[&controls::Saturation] = ControlInfo(0.0f, 1.993f, defaultSaturation_);
 
 	/* Hue adjustment is negated by kHueScale, min/max are swapped */
 	cmap[&controls::Hue] = ControlInfo(HueQ::TraitsType::max * kHueScale,
@@ -113,12 +122,12 @@
 {
 	auto &cproc = context.activeState.cproc;
 
-	cproc.requestedBrightness = kDefaultBrightness;
-	cproc.contrast = ContrastQ(kDefaultContrast);
+	cproc.requestedBrightness = defaultBrightness_;
+	cproc.contrast = ContrastQ(defaultContrast_);
 	applyRequestedBrightness(context.activeState);
 
 	cproc.hue = HueQ(kDefaultHue);
-	cproc.saturation = SaturationQ(kDefaultSaturation);
+	cproc.saturation = SaturationQ(defaultSaturation_);
 
 	return 0;
 }
diff -ruN cp72_a/src/ipa/rkisp1/algorithms/cproc.h cp72_b/src/ipa/rkisp1/algorithms/cproc.h
--- a/src/ipa/rkisp1/algorithms/cproc.h
+++ b/src/ipa/rkisp1/algorithms/cproc.h
@@ -34,6 +34,11 @@
 		     IPAFrameContext &frameContext,
 		     const rkisp1_stat_buffer *stats,
 		     ControlList &metadata) override;
+
+private:
+	float defaultBrightness_;
+	float defaultContrast_;
+	float defaultSaturation_;
 };
 
 } /* namespace ipa::rkisp1::algorithms */
